command: sed -n '1,220p' README_en.md
command: sed -n '1,260p' harness/README.md
command: nl -ba harness/include/metrics.h | sed -n '1,220p'
command: nl -ba harness/src/consumer.cpp | sed -n '1,260p'

finding: metrics::Accumulator does not use arrival order or a running last-seq gap counter to compute expected or dropped.
metrics.h:31: void record(uint64_t seq_id, uint64_t latency_ns) {
metrics.h:32:   if (received_ == 0) {
metrics.h:33:     first_seq_ = seq_id;
metrics.h:34:     last_seq_ = seq_id;
metrics.h:36:     if (seq_id < first_seq_) first_seq_ = seq_id;
metrics.h:37:     if (seq_id > last_seq_) last_seq_ = seq_id;
metrics.h:49: r.expected = last_seq_ - first_seq_ + 1;
metrics.h:50: r.dropped = r.expected > r.received ? r.expected - r.received : 0;
consumer.cpp:115: acc.record(hdr->seq_id, latency);

consequence: publishing frames in arrival order can make seq_id arrive out of order, but expected remains max_seen_seq - min_seen_seq + 1 and dropped remains expected - received. Adjacent swaps and bounded reordering do not by themselves create false drops.
change_needed: no metrics.h change is needed.
previous_numbers_changed: no, because no measurement-side code was changed.
judgement: rejected a running gap-counter fix because the implementation already uses max/min sequence accounting.
